CHAPTER XII
INVESTIGATION
Article 23 (1) In addition to general investigation officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation may also be carried out by Civil Servant Investigators whose appointment is in accordance with the legislation. (2) Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take initial action at that time at the scene and conduct examination; c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identity;
d. seize objects and/or documents; e. take fingerprints and photograph a person; f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in expert witnesses required in connection with the examination of the case;
h. terminate the investigation after receiving instruction from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
(3) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2), shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigating Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 26
In the case of trial proceedings related to cases of violence against children, the presence of the child does not necessarily have to be presented at the trial, except in circumstances that are very compelling while still separating the meeting between the child and the perpetrator in trial processes at different times.

Article 27
In the case where the perpetrator of a criminal act is a child, the following matters apply:
a. the legal process is carried out humanely by considering that the child still has hope for their future;
b. The investigator must be a person who has competence in special investigation for child handling cases;
c. The investigation is conducted in a closed room and only attended by the advocacy team and the child's parents/guardian;
d. in the investigation process, the child is not placed in general detention, but rather entrusted to their family with supervision and safeguarding;
e. the trial process is carried out specifically in a juvenile court room; and
f. the sentencing process for the child is placed in a special juvenile correctional institution, with a transition period that can be accompanied by their parents.

